From 1f2e380cc1e05d3275972ec660155e174f5c98b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 22:33:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/25] meson: detect whether the C library needs _GNU_SOURCE
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The block deciding _GNU_SOURCE is a list of operating system names, and
carries Mesa's own "TODO: this is very incomplete" above it. What it is
really asking is a property of the C library: whether GNU extensions
such as asprintf and mkostemp are declared only when _GNU_SOURCE is
defined. An OS name cannot answer that.

Ask the C library. When the chain has not already defined _GNU_SOURCE,
compile a probe with and without it; define it only when doing so
demonstrably changes whether the extension is declared. The probe takes
the function's address rather than calling it, so a missing declaration
is an error in every C dialect rather than a warning some compiler
versions let through.

Nothing already working changes. A libc that declares these anyway (the
BSDs, macOS) makes the first compile succeed and the block does
nothing; Solaris, which uses __EXTENSIONS__, fails both probes and is
left alone; Windows is excluded, and MinGW has already added
_GNU_SOURCE by the time this is reached.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 3 item 3 (newlib/libnx gaps)
Why: newlib declares asprintf and mkostemp under __GNU_VISIBLE, i.e.
  only with _GNU_SOURCE, and 'horizon' is not on Mesa's list. The
  functions are present — configure finds mkostemp by link test — so
  this is a visibility gap, not a missing-function gap, which is
  exactly why an OS list gets it wrong.
Evidence: building src/util for aarch64/horizon fails with
    anon_file.c:86:9: error: implicit declaration of function
    'mkostemp'; did you mean 'mkstemp'?
    anon_file.c:122:13: error: implicit declaration of function
    'asprintf'; did you mean 'asnprintf'?
  while the same configure reports Checking for function "mkostemp":
  YES. With this patch configure reports
    Checking if "GNU extensions declared by default" compiles: NO
    Checking if "GNU extensions declared with _GNU_SOURCE" compiles: YES
  and -D_GNU_SOURCE reaches every compile line.
Upstream: yes — it answers the TODO the code already carries, and is a
  no-op on every platform currently on the list.
---
 meson.build | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/meson.build b/meson.build
index ddec752..ef415aa 100644
--- a/meson.build
+++ b/meson.build
@@ -1248,6 +1248,28 @@ elif host_machine.system() == 'openbsd'
   pre_args += '-D_ISOC11_SOURCE'
 endif
 
+# The list above is a list of operating systems standing in for a
+# property of the C library: whether it hides GNU extensions such as
+# asprintf and mkostemp unless _GNU_SOURCE is defined. Ask the C library
+# instead of adding another name to it. The probe takes the function's
+# address rather than calling it, so a missing declaration is an error
+# in every C dialect, not a warning some compilers let through.
+#
+# Only reached when the chain above did not already define _GNU_SOURCE,
+# and it only acts when defining it demonstrably changes the answer, so
+# a C library that declares these anyway (the BSDs, macOS) or that uses
+# a different macro (Solaris' __EXTENSIONS__) is left exactly as it was.
+if not pre_args.contains('-D_GNU_SOURCE') and host_machine.system() != 'windows'
+  _gnu_ext_probe = '''
+    #include <stdio.h>
+    int main(void) { return (void *)&asprintf != (void *)0; }'''
+  if not cc.compiles(_gnu_ext_probe, name : 'GNU extensions declared by default') and \
+     cc.compiles(_gnu_ext_probe, args : '-D_GNU_SOURCE',
+                 name : 'GNU extensions declared with _GNU_SOURCE')
+    pre_args += '-D_GNU_SOURCE'
+  endif
+endif
+
 # Check for generic C arguments
 c_msvc_compat_args = []
 no_override_init_args = []
